{"author":{"uri":{"label":"https://itunes.apple.com/us/reviews/id576691320"}, "name":{"label":"amb3rry"}, "label":""}, "updated":{"label":"2025-06-21T19:23:58-07:00"}, "im:rating":{"label":"1"}, "im:version":{"label":"4.2.5"}, "id":{"label":"12802473827"}, "title":{"label":"A Cash Grab Disguised as an App – Rizz is a Complete Letdown"}, "content":{"label":"I usually don’t write reviews, but my experience with the Rizz app was frustrating enough that I felt the need to warn others before they waste their time or money. This app is a textbook example of a modern bait-and-switch – promising innovative features, sleek design, and helpful tools, but hiding everything even remotely useful behind an aggressive paywall.\n\nLet me break it down:\n\n1. Absolutely No Free Functionality\n\nThe most glaring issue with Rizz is that there is nothing – and I mean nothing – useful you can do on this app without paying. The app markets itself as some kind of revolutionary social AI assistant or “rizz generator” (whatever that means), but the moment you open it, you’re greeted with pop-ups to upgrade or subscribe. You can’t even test the core functionality to see if it’s worth it. No trial, no basic version, no limited access. Just a brick wall with a price tag on it.\n\nIf you’re expecting even a basic feature set to be free – like a few messages, sample profiles, or basic prompts – forget about it. The “free” version exists in name only. It’s a glorified advertisement for their premium plan.\n\n2. Shady Monetization Strategy\n\nRizz isn’t just subscription-based – it pushes you into premium tiers with outrageous pricing. You’ll be asked to cough up a monthly fee that’s comparable to full-featured productivity software like Notion, ChatGPT Plus, or Adobe tools – but for what? A chatbot that spits out generic compliments and recycled flirty one-liners?\n\nThere are constant prompts, misleading buttons, and sneaky UI tricks to get you to tap “Subscribe.” Some buttons look like regular features, but tapping them immediately leads to a payment page. It feels predatory, like the developers prioritized squeezing money out of users over offering any genuine value.\n\n3. Misleading Marketing\n\nThe app’s description and promotional material make it sound like it will enhance your dating life with AI-powered conversation help, message templates, pickup lines, and smooth responses. But the reality is a watered-down, poorly executed chatbot with painfully limited use unless you pay. Even then, the premium features don’t deliver anything you couldn’t find online for free.\n\nTheir “AI” is no more intelligent than a random Instagram caption generator. And no, it doesn’t “learn from your conversations” – that’s just fluff to make the tech sound smarter than it is.\n\n4. Poor User Experience\n\nAside from the paywall nightmare, the app itself is clunky and slow. Navigating between tabs is laggy, and half the time it takes several seconds just to load a simple menu. Notifications don’t work properly, and there are frequent crashes. The UI is overly flashy but unintuitive, with confusing layouts that seem more interested in looking “modern” than actually being usable.\n\n5. No Value for Money\n\nEven if you do decide to shell out cash, you’ll quickly realize how shallow the content is. There’s a small collection of recycled templates and generic messages that feel like they were scraped from Reddit in 2018. The AI doesn’t adapt, doesn’t learn, and doesn’t impress. If you’re looking for genuine help with conversations or flirting, you’d be better off reading a few free blog articles or watching YouTube videos – at least those have some depth and variety.\n\n6. Support is Useless\n\nIf you think contacting support will help, think again. There’s no real customer service – just a chatbot with auto-replies. I reached out about the misleading “free” access and received nothing but canned responses and an unhelpful link to their FAQ. No refunds, no accountability, no dialogue.\n\n⸻\n\nFinal Verdict: 1/5 Stars (And that’s generous)\n\nRizz is a perfect example of everything that’s wrong with mobile apps in 2025. It promises innovation but delivers frustration. It hides basic functionality behind an aggressive paywall. It pretends to be smart but ends up being generic. It offers no trial, no transparency, and no reason to trust it.\n\nUnless you’re interested in paying a premium subscription just to test whether it’s worth anything (spoiler: it’s not), I strongly recommend avoiding this app. There are far better tools out there – many of them free – that actually help with communication, relationships, or self-confidence.\n\nDon’t fall for the hype. Rizz is all marketing and no substance.", "attributes":{"type":"text"}}, "link":{"attributes":{"rel":"related", "href":"https://itunes.apple.com/us/review?id=1663430725&type=Purple%20Software"}}, "im:voteSum":{"label":"2"}, "im:contentType":{"attributes":{"term":"Application", "label":"Application"}}, "im:voteCount":{"label":"3"}}, 
